{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790528520,"nonce":"GGSqq5eF0g5AB+XyA94gxGR/ffk=","title":"Window plugin: a web backend over the browser's canvas and DOM events","message":"In a page, cb_window's members and events map to browser APIs: plugins/cb_window/spec/window.idl vendors them, verbatim from the specifications' IDL (webref 89e7d68), and its README maps each member and event. Design: a window agent on the page's thread listens on the \u003ccanvas\u003e element and the document and writes events into a queue in the program's shared memory, which poll() reads without a call into JavaScript; the program's requests (title, cursor, fullscreen, size, IME area via EditContext) go over a wire generated from the IDL. The OffscreenCanvas lives in the GPU agent after transferControlToOffscreen, so a size change has to reach its owner for width/height, while the element's CSS size is the page's. Gesture-gated requests (fullscreen, pointer lock, focus) are carried out in the next input event's handler. Needs a page-to-program event direction on the wire (today the wire carries program-to-agent calls and replies), and Ash's page to host the window agent on its thread.","files":null}]}